WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.180
<v Maya>Welcome to part five.

2
00:00:02.230 --> 00:00:05.350
<v Maya>Last time we met the master table and the splits.

3
00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:09.710
<v Maya>Today is the heart of the lecture, the research that explains the set numbers.

4
00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:13.210
<v Maya>Each study gives a takeaway, and each takeaway is a factor.

5
00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:15.640
<v Maya>Factor one is stimulus per set.

6
00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:17.590
<v Dev>Stimulus meaning what?

7
00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:21.680
<v Maya>The hypertrophy stimulus, from the word stimulation, meaning a change.

8
00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:26.540
<v Maya>The question is, if you do one set for a muscle, how much growth stimulus does it get?

9
00:00:26.590 --> 00:00:30.400
<v Maya>The researchers measured it in arbitrary units, not kilograms.

10
00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:34.300
<v Maya>To make it relatable, the lecture showed one unit as one rupee.

11
00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:37.340
<v Maya>That is only an analogy, not a real measurement.

12
00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:39.950
<v Dev>So one set equals one rupee.

13
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:40.840
<v Maya>Yes.

14
00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:44.730
<v Maya>Now the table, as net stimulus, the total after that many sets.

15
00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:47.110
<v Maya>One set, one point zero zero.

16
00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:49.270
<v Maya>Two sets, one point three nine.

17
00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:51.340
<v Maya>Three sets, one point six one.

18
00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:53.690
<v Maya>Four sets, one point seven seven.

19
00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:56.050
<v Maya>Five sets, one point nine zero.

20
00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:58.550
<v Maya>Six sets, two point zero zero.

21
00:00:58.600 --> 00:00:59.690
<v Dev>Hold on.

22
00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:02.930
<v Dev>If one set gives one, shouldn't two sets give two?

23
00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:04.960
<v Maya>That is exactly the surprise.

24
00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:10.240
<v Maya>Two sets give only one point three nine, and three sets give one point six one, not three.

25
00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:13.150
<v Maya>It takes six sets to double what the first set gave.

26
00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.600
<v Maya>Five more sets to earn one more unit.

27
00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:18.330
<v Dev>And the third column, stimulus per set?

28
00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:20.020
<v Maya>That is what each set added.

29
00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:23.030
<v Maya>It is the difference between consecutive totals.

30
00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:27.440
<v Maya>Set two, one point three nine minus one equals zero point three nine.

31
00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:32.640
<v Maya>Set three, one point six one minus one point three nine, equals zero point two two.

32
00:01:32.690 --> 00:01:38.100
<v Maya>Set four, one point seven seven minus one point six one, is zero point one six.

33
00:01:38.150 --> 00:01:43.400
<v Maya>Set five, one point nine zero minus one point seven seven, zero point one three.

34
00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:47.730
<v Maya>Set six, two minus one point nine zero, zero point one zero.

35
00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:51.670
<v Dev>A common mistake would be subtracting from one every time.

36
00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:54.010
<v Maya>Yes, and the lecture warns about it.

37
00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:57.850
<v Maya>For set three it is minus one point three nine, not minus one.

38
00:01:57.900 --> 00:01:59.210
<v Maya>Now a neat check.

39
00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:00.860
<v Maya>Add sets two to six.

40
00:02:00.910 --> 00:02:11.020
<v Maya>Zero point three nine, plus zero point two two, plus zero point one six, plus zero point one three, plus zero point one zero equals one point zero zero.

41
00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:14.640
<v Maya>So five sets together add the same as the first set alone.

42
00:02:14.690 --> 00:02:18.200
<v Dev>The lecture said roughly one point zero two, didn't it?

43
00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:21.480
<v Maya>It did, but the exact sum is one point zero zero.

44
00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:24.290
<v Maya>Rounding in the published numbers could explain it.

45
00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:31.560
<v Maya>Beyond six sets, seven sets about two point zero nine, eight about two point one six, nine about two point two three.

46
00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:33.460
<v Maya>Each extra set adds less.

47
00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:39.200
<v Maya>The seventh adds about zero point zero nine and the eighth about zero point zero seven.

48
00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:42.910
<v Dev>So the total keeps rising, but each set is worth less.

49
00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:47.060
<v Maya>That is the law of diminishing returns, an idea from economics.

50
00:02:47.110 --> 00:02:49.130
<v Maya>The more the merrier is not always true.

51
00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:50.420
<v Maya>Picture the graph.

52
00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:53.620
<v Maya>Number of sets along the bottom, stimulus up the side.

53
00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:57.790
<v Maya>If doubling held, two sets would reach two, and the line would shoot up steeply.

54
00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:02.630
<v Maya>Instead the curve rises quickly at first, then flattens like a hill levelling off.

55
00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:04.610
<v Dev>And it never goes down?

56
00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:06.550
<v Maya>Eventually it takes a U turn.

57
00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:08.810
<v Maya>More sets means more muscle damage.

58
00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:11.030
<v Maya>Draw damage as a second curve.

59
00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:14.490
<v Maya>It stays near zero for the first few sets, then rises.

60
00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:17.590
<v Maya>When damage matters, it pulls the net stimulus down.

61
00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:22.410
<v Maya>So the net stimulus becomes an inverted U, a hill with a peak and then a descent.

62
00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:25.110
<v Dev>Is the damage the same for every muscle?

63
00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:25.950
<v Maya>No.

64
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:29.290
<v Maya>Some muscles are more easily damaged and others less.

65
00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:42.660
<v Maya>In the published graph, a more easily damaged muscle reaches about one point seven seven at four sets, rises a little to about two at five and six sets, then falls back to about one point seven seven by nine sets.

66
00:03:42.710 --> 00:03:46.780
<v Maya>The extra five sets added nothing, so four were enough for that muscle.

67
00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:48.670
<v Dev>And the less damaged one?

68
00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:52.430
<v Maya>Rises to about two at six sets and is still about two at nine.

69
00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:56.760
<v Maya>The extra three sets are unnecessary, so stopping at six was enough.

70
00:03:56.810 --> 00:03:59.370
<v Dev>So that is why we have upper limits.

71
00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:00.510
<v Maya>Exactly.

72
00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:04.010
<v Maya>Max three, max five, max ten in the set range column.

73
00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:11.300
<v Maya>In the third scenario more than five sets could mean twenty or twenty five sets, so five is the minimum and a cap is needed.

74
00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:12.260
<v Maya>Recap.

75
00:04:12.310 --> 00:04:13.830
<v Maya>One set gives one.

76
00:04:13.880 --> 00:04:20.320
<v Maya>Totals are one, one point three nine, one point six one, one point seven seven, one point nine, two.

77
00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:22.130
<v Maya>Each set adds the difference.

78
00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:24.650
<v Maya>Six sets are needed to double one set.

79
00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:27.540
<v Maya>Too many sets bring damage, giving an inverted U.

80
00:04:27.590 --> 00:04:28.740
<v Maya>Pause and think.

81
00:04:28.790 --> 00:04:32.410
<v Maya>What does the third set add, and which total do you subtract from?

82
00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:37.600
<v Dev>One point six one minus one point three nine, which is zero point two two.

83
00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:39.260
<v Dev>Not minus one.

84
00:04:39.310 --> 00:04:40.400
<v Maya>Exactly.

85
00:04:40.450 --> 00:04:41.630
<v Maya>On to factor two.

86
00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:44.910
<v Maya>Once you earn a stimulus, how long does the muscle keep it?

87
00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:48.380
<v Maya>It is not a fixed deposit, like money in a bank locker.

88
00:04:48.430 --> 00:04:53.900
<v Maya>If the muscle is not trained again in time, atrophy sets in and the stimulus falls to zero.

89
00:04:53.950 --> 00:04:56.370
<v Maya>This is stimulus retention per set.

90
00:04:56.420 --> 00:04:57.850
<v Dev>How many days?

91
00:04:57.900 --> 00:04:59.980
<v Maya>Take the training day as day one.

92
00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:03.960
<v Maya>One set, one point zero zero, reaches zero on day five.

93
00:05:04.010 --> 00:05:05.550
<v Maya>Two sets, day six.

94
00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:07.150
<v Maya>Three sets, day seven.

95
00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:08.730
<v Maya>Four sets, day eight.

96
00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:10.310
<v Maya>Five sets, day nine.

97
00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:12.030
<v Maya>Six sets, day ten.

98
00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:13.430
<v Dev>There's a pattern.

99
00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:15.070
<v Dev>Sets plus four.

100
00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:15.960
<v Maya>Yes.

101
00:05:16.010 --> 00:05:21.790
<v Maya>Add four to the number of sets, and that is the day the stimulus reaches zero if you do not train again.

102
00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:24.250
<v Maya>Each extra set buys about one more day.

103
00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:26.070
<v Maya>These are not fixed numbers.

104
00:05:26.120 --> 00:05:31.220
<v Maya>Some studies say four days for one set and others five, so the lecture picked the middle.

105
00:05:31.270 --> 00:05:34.740
<v Dev>Does it depend on beginner, intermediate or advanced?

106
00:05:34.790 --> 00:05:35.580
<v Maya>No.

107
00:05:35.630 --> 00:05:37.380
<v Maya>It applies to every level.

108
00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:40.170
<v Maya>Reps are already set around stimulating reps.

109
00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:46.850
<v Maya>A four to six RM weight differs from person to person, but the stimulus each earns stays for the same number of days.

110
00:05:46.900 --> 00:05:52.310
<v Maya>Now combine the factors, using the latissimus dorsi, though it applies to every muscle.

111
00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:55.430
<v Maya>Full body, one to three sets, lats three times.

112
00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:57.530
<v Maya>Upper lower, Monday and Thursday.

113
00:05:57.580 --> 00:06:00.740
<v Maya>Push pull legs, once, Monday and next Monday.

114
00:06:00.790 --> 00:06:04.640
<v Dev>What goes wrong with once a week and only one or two sets?

115
00:06:04.690 --> 00:06:07.010
<v Maya>One set reaches zero on day five.

116
00:06:07.060 --> 00:06:08.700
<v Maya>Two sets, on day six.

117
00:06:08.750 --> 00:06:10.430
<v Maya>Next Monday is day eight.

118
00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:16.270
<v Maya>Everything earned has gone to zero, so the muscle starts from scratch every week, with nothing carried forward.

119
00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:18.450
<v Maya>You gain, lose and start again.

120
00:06:18.500 --> 00:06:23.430
<v Maya>You do not have to carry all of one point seven seven forward, but you must carry something.

121
00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:25.670
<v Maya>That is how muscle size builds.

122
00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:28.670
<v Dev>And the person on full body with two sets?

123
00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:32.220
<v Maya>One point three nine would take until day six to reach zero.

124
00:06:32.270 --> 00:06:36.360
<v Maya>But the muscle is trained again on Wednesday and Friday, long before zero.

125
00:06:36.410 --> 00:06:38.310
<v Maya>They push it up before it falls.

126
00:06:38.360 --> 00:06:41.160
<v Maya>That person cannot do two sets once a week.

127
00:06:41.210 --> 00:06:42.360
<v Maya>Pause and think.

128
00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:46.960
<v Maya>Why can a thrice a week lifter use two sets, but a once a week lifter cannot?

129
00:06:47.010 --> 00:06:57.530
<v Dev>Because the thrice a week lifter trains again before the stimulus reaches zero, and the once a week lifter with two sets reaches zero on day six, before the next session.

130
00:06:57.580 --> 00:06:58.590
<v Maya>Yes.

131
00:06:58.640 --> 00:07:01.920
<v Dev>So the once a week person needs about six sets.

132
00:07:01.970 --> 00:07:02.810
<v Maya>Yes.

133
00:07:02.860 --> 00:07:05.890
<v Maya>Six sets, two point zero, last until day ten.

134
00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:08.410
<v Maya>Next session is day eight, before zero.

135
00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:14.390
<v Maya>The lecture's example said about zero point four was still left, an illustrative figure from the board.

136
00:07:14.440 --> 00:07:16.850
<v Maya>That balance is carried into the new session.

137
00:07:16.900 --> 00:07:19.680
<v Maya>So the rule is, lower frequency, more sets.

138
00:07:19.730 --> 00:07:21.830
<v Maya>Higher frequency, fewer sets.

139
00:07:21.880 --> 00:07:27.250
<v Maya>And do not choose the high number of sets at high frequency either, the person would just get tired.

140
00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:31.090
<v Dev>Doesn't the full body person lose out with just two sets?

141
00:07:31.140 --> 00:07:33.140
<v Maya>Compare the same six sets a week.

142
00:07:33.190 --> 00:07:35.600
<v Maya>Full body, two sets three times.

143
00:07:35.650 --> 00:07:44.390
<v Maya>One point three nine, three times, is one point three nine plus one point three nine plus one point three nine equals four point one seven.

144
00:07:44.440 --> 00:07:46.460
<v Maya>Upper lower, three sets twice.

145
00:07:46.510 --> 00:07:50.820
<v Maya>One point six one plus one point six one equals three point two two.

146
00:07:50.870 --> 00:07:53.960
<v Maya>Once a week, six sets, two point zero zero.

147
00:07:54.010 --> 00:07:56.970
<v Dev>So three times a week wins, by a lot.

148
00:07:57.020 --> 00:07:57.860
<v Maya>Yes.

149
00:07:57.910 --> 00:08:01.830
<v Maya>Four point one seven, three point two two, two point zero zero.

150
00:08:01.880 --> 00:08:04.690
<v Maya>Full body is not underrated for no reason.

151
00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:08.100
<v Maya>But this does not make push pull legs or bro split bad.

152
00:08:08.150 --> 00:08:11.430
<v Maya>Professional bodybuilders must focus on every muscle.

153
00:08:11.480 --> 00:08:19.610
<v Maya>A full body session of ten to twelve exercises has no room for niche exercises like reverse shrugs for the lower trapezius.

154
00:08:19.660 --> 00:08:21.850
<v Maya>A dedicated day gives that room.

155
00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:25.980
<v Maya>The lecture ranks nothing, it only shows how to understand the three categories.

156
00:08:26.030 --> 00:08:27.650
<v Maya>Five things to remember.

157
00:08:27.700 --> 00:08:35.190
<v Maya>One, stimulus per set: totals one, one point three nine, one point six one, one point seven seven, one point nine, two.

158
00:08:35.240 --> 00:08:36.850
<v Maya>Each set adds less.

159
00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:44.400
<v Dev>Two, six sets double one set, and damage turns the curve into an inverted U, which is why there are upper limits.

160
00:08:44.450 --> 00:08:50.050
<v Maya>Three, a stimulus reaches zero on day sets plus four, counting the training day as day one.

161
00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:54.880
<v Dev>Four, lower frequency means more sets, higher frequency can use fewer.

162
00:08:54.930 --> 00:09:03.880
<v Maya>Five, net weekly stimulus for six sets: four point one seven for three times a week, three point two two for twice, two point zero zero for once.

163
00:09:03.930 --> 00:09:08.850
<v Maya>Next time, hypertrophy and atrophy, the ideal ranges, and sample programmes.

